  "answer": "To reduce the risk of a reinforcement learning agent irreversibly disrupting parts of its environment that are not represented in its reward function (i.e., task-irrelevant parts), researchers have proposed fine-tuning the agent with an Attainable Utility Preservation (AUP) penalty (https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/5kurn5W62C5CpSWq6/avoiding-side-effects-in-complex-environments). \n\nThis mechanism works by sampling one or more random auxiliary reward functions—such as a single latent-dimensional auxiliary goal learned unsupervised—and augmenting the agent's task reward with an AUP penalty during the training phase (https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/5kurn5W62C5CpSWq6/avoiding-side-effects-in-complex-environments). The penalty punishes the agent for actions that significantly change its ability to achieve these random auxiliary goals (https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/5kurn5W62C5CpSWq6/avoiding-side-effects-in-complex-environments). By doing so, it keeps the agent's overall attainable utility landscape stable, which induces conservative behavior and steers the policy to complete its specific task without unnecessarily altering or damaging the rest of the environment (https://www.alignmentforum.org/posts/5kurn5W62C5CpSWq6/avoiding-side-effects-in-complex-environments)."
